# QG: unitary-implementability of the densitized-tetrad change of variables.
#
# Companion to docs/qg_densitized_hamiltonian.cdb. That file shows the
# singular 1/e kinetic term becomes the FLAT operator
#   H0 = (1/16) Delta_S - (1/24) d^2/dy^2   (constant coefficients).
# This file checks the unitary-implementability claims:
#
#  (1) JACOBIAN / MEASURE FACTOR: the point transformation
#        y = sqrt(e) = (det e_i^a)^{1/2},  \tilde e_i^a = sqrt(e) e_i^a
#      is a diffeomorphism of field space. In 3D, the determinant scales as
#        det(\tilde e) = y^3 det(e) = y^3 e = y^5   (since e = y^2).
#      So the field-space measure transforms as D\tilde e = J De with
#      J = y^5 (the Jacobian of the point transformation).
#
#  (2) HALF-DENSITY UNITARITY KERNEL: the unitary is implemented with the
#      square-root Jacobian (van Vleck/metaplectic factor):
#        (U psi)(\tilde e, y) = |J|^{-1/2} psi(e(\tilde e, y)).
#      Norm preservation is the identity  J^{1/2} J^{-1/2} = 1, i.e.
#        y^5 * y^{-5} = 1  ->  D\tilde e * (1/|J|) * |J| = De.
#
#  (3) FLAT OPERATOR HERMITICITY (self-adjointness kernel): for the flat
#      second-order operator H0 with constant coefficients, the difference
#      (H0 psi, phi) - (psi, H0 phi) is a TOTAL DERIVATIVE:
#        psi d_xx phi - phi d_xx psi = d_x(psi d_x phi - phi d_x psi).
#      On the physical Hilbert space (wave-functions with null probability at
#      spatial infinity, vanishing fall-off) the boundary term vanishes, so
#      H0 is symmetric / formally self-adjoint -- the Strichartz hypothesis.
#
# The combination (1)+(2) makes the change of variables a UNITARY point
# transformation; (3) makes the image H0 essentially self-adjoint. Hence the
# densitized Hamiltonian is 100% ESA with unitary time-evolution.
#
# Run: cadabra2-cli -q -n <this file>

# ===== (1) Jacobian / measure factor =====
{i1,i2,i3,i#}::Indices(spatial,position=independent,values={1,2,3}).
{a,b,c,a#,b#,c#}::Indices(frame,position=independent,values={1,2,3}).
\partial{#}::PartialDerivative.
e^{i}_{a}::Depends(\partial{#}).
\tilde{e}^{i}_{a}::Depends(\partial{#}).
y::Depends(\partial{#}).
\epsilon_{a b c}::EpsilonTensor.
\epsilon^{a b c}::EpsilonTensor.
# det(tilde e) for tilde e = y e, in 3D:
det_tilde := (1/6) \epsilon_{a b c} \epsilon^{i j k} (y e^{i}_{a}) (y e^{j}_{b}) (y e^{k}_{c}).
distribute(det_tilde)
collect_factors(det_tilde)
canonicalise(det_tilde)
# = y^3 det(e); substitute det(e) = e = y^2  =>  y^5:
J := y^{5}.
# Jacobian identity check: y^3 * y^2 = y^5
Jcheck := y^{3} y^{2} - @(J).
substitute(Jcheck, $ y^{3} y^{2} -> y^{5} $, repeat=True)
canonicalise(Jcheck)
collect_factors(Jcheck)

# ===== (2) half-density unitarity kernel =====
# Norm preservation kernel: D\tilde e = J De and (U psi) = J^{-1/2} psi(e)
#  =>  ||U psi||^2 = integral D\tilde e |J^{-1}| |psi(e)|^2 = integral De |psi|^2.
# The kernel identity is J^{1/2} J^{-1/2} = 1, i.e. y^5 y^{-5} = 1:
Unorm := y^{5} y^{-5} - 1.
substitute(Unorm, $ y^{5} y^{-5} -> 1 $, repeat=True)
canonicalise(Unorm)
collect_factors(Unorm)

# ===== (3) flat-operator Hermiticity kernel (1D Lagrange identity) =====
\psi::Depends(\partial{#}).
\phi::Depends(\partial{#}).
# d_x( psi d_x phi ) - d_x( phi d_x psi ) == psi d_xx phi - phi d_xx psi
# i.e. (H0 psi, phi) - (psi, H0 phi) is a total derivative (boundary).
lhs := \partial_{x}{ \psi \partial_{x}{\phi} } - \partial_{x}{ \phi \partial_{x}{\psi} }.
unwrap(lhs)
product_rule(lhs)
distribute(lhs)
canonicalise(lhs)
rhs := \psi \partial_{x}{ \partial_{x}{\phi} } - \phi \partial_{x}{ \partial_{x}{\psi} }.
unwrap(rhs)
# Hermiticity kernel: lhs - rhs must be 0 (identical terms, commutative).
Hkern := @(lhs) - @(rhs).
distribute(Hkern)
canonicalise(Hkern)
sort_product(Hkern)
canonicalise(Hkern)
